## Why This Is Asked
Dynamic maximum XOR subset queries with deletions present a non-trivial challenge. An offline approach using interval decomposition elegantly handles deletions by converting them into active intervals.

## Key Concepts
- Time-segment tree over operation timeline
- Rollbackable linear basis for XOR operations
- Handling duplicates through separate active intervals

## Code Example

```javascript
class LinearBasis {
  constructor() { 
    this.b = new Array(32).fill(0); 
    this.history = [];
  }

  insert(x) {
    const original = [...this.b];
    this.history.push(original);

    for (let i = 31; i >= 0; i--) {
      if (!(x >> i) & 1) continue;
      if (!this.b[i]) {
        this.b[i] = x;
        break;
      }
      x ^= this.b[i];
    }
  }

  rollback() {
    this.b = this.history.pop();
  }

  maxXor() {
    let result = 0;
    for (let i = 31; i >= 0; i--) {
      result = Math.max(result, result ^ this.b[i]);
    }
    return result;
  }
}

class SegmentTree {
  constructor(n) {
    this.size = 1;
    while (this.size < n) this.size *= 2;
    this.tree = Array(2 * this.size).fill().map(() => []);
  }

  addInterval(l, r, value) {
    for (l += this.size, r += this.size; l < r; l >>= 1, r >>= 1) {
      if (l & 1) this.tree[l++].push(value);
      if (r & 1) this.tree[--r].push(value);
    }
  }

  process(queries) {
    const results = [];
    const basis = new LinearBasis();

    const dfs = (node, nodeL, nodeR) => {
      const saveHistorySize = basis.history.length;

      for (const value of this.tree[node]) {
        basis.insert(value);
      }

      if (nodeR - nodeL === 1) {
        results[nodeL] = basis.maxXor();
      } else {
        const mid = (nodeL + nodeR) / 2;
        dfs(node * 2, nodeL, mid);
        dfs(node * 2 + 1, mid, nodeR);
      }

      while (basis.history.length > saveHistorySize) {
        basis.rollback();
      }
    };

    dfs(1, 0, this.size);
    return results;
  }
}
```

## Update Rules
- Insert(x): Map to interval [time, ∞) until erase
- Erase(x): Close interval at current time
- Query: Process at leaf, gather basis maxXor result
- Rollback: Restore basis to previous state after subtree processing

## Complexity
- Build: O(n log n) for interval decomposition
- Process: O(32 log n) per query (32-bit basis × segment tree depth)
- Memory: O(n log n) for storing intervals in segment tree

This solution efficiently handles the dynamic nature with deletions by leveraging interval decomposition and rollback mechanisms, providing optimal query performance while maintaining clean separation of concerns.

## Why This Is Asked
This question simulates a live order book and tests dynamic range sums alongside top-k queries within a price band. It combines segment trees with per-node order statistics and requires careful handling of cancellations to maintain consistent state.

## Key Concepts
- **Dynamic segment tree** over the price domain for efficient range queries
- **Per-node multisets** to support kthLargest queries with duplicate prices
- **Order ID mapping** (idMap) for O(1) updates and cancellations
- **Amortized O(log² n)** operations: O(log n) tree traversal × O(log n) multiset operations

## Memory Handling
The idMap stores each active order's metadata, enabling O(1) lookups for updates and cancellations. When an order is cancelled, its entry is removed from both the idMap and the relevant multiset, ensuring no memory leaks. The segment tree nodes are created lazily on demand, only when prices are actually inserted.

## Cancellations
Cancellations require removing a specific quantity from a price node's multiset—O(log n) for the multiset operation plus O(log n) for updating the segment tree. This maintains accurate totals for both sum and kthLargest queries.

## Code Example
