## Why This Is Asked

Autocomplete-like prefix queries with dynamic updates are common in databases and search systems. A Trie with counts cleanly supports insertions, deletions, and fast prefix queries, even with duplicates.

## Key Concepts

- Trie with per-node pass-through counts
- O(len(word)) insertion/erasure; O(len(prefix)) prefix queries
- Correct handling of duplicates via counts, not just terminal markers

## Code Example

```javascript
class TrieNode {
  constructor() {
    this.next = new Map();
    this.cnt = 0;
    this.end = false;
  }
}

class WordTrie {
  constructor() { this.root = new TrieNode(); }

  insertWord(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) node.next.set(ch, new TrieNode());
      node = node.next.get(ch);
      node.cnt++;
    }
    node.end = true;
  }

  eraseWord(word) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of word) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return;
      node = node.next.get(ch);
      node.cnt--;
    }
    node.end = false;
  }

  countWordsWithPrefix(prefix) {
    let node = this.root;
    for (let ch of prefix) {
      if (!node.next.has(ch)) return 0;
      node = node.next.get(ch);
    }
    return node.cnt;
  }
}
```

## How It Works

- **insertWord**: Traverses/creates nodes for each character, incrementing the pass-through count at each node
- **eraseWord**: Traverses existing nodes, decrementing counts; nodes with zero count can be pruned
- **countWordsWithPrefix**: Traverses to the prefix node and returns its pass-through count, representing all words that pass through this prefix

The pass-through count at each node represents how many words in the trie have that prefix, enabling O(len(prefix)) prefix queries while maintaining O(len(word)) updates.

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design an order-statistics structure with duplicates using a compact, well-known DS (Fenwick). Requires handling inserts, deletes, and k-th retrieval within tight bounds.

## Key Concepts

- Coordinate compression to bound domain size
- Fenwick Tree for prefix sums (counts)
- getKthSmallest via binary lifting on Fenwick
- Handling duplicates and safe deletes
- Time: O(log m) per operation, m distinct values

## Code Example

```javascript
class Fenwick {
  constructor(n){ this.n=n; this.f=new Array(n+1).fill(0); }
  add(i, delta){ for(; i<=this.n; i+= i&-i) this.f[i]+=delta; }
  sum(i){ let s=0; for(; i>0; i-= i&-i) s+=this.f[i]; return s; }
  kth(k){
    let idx=0; let bit=1;
    while((bit<<1) <= this.n) bit <<= 1;
    for(let step=bit; step>0; step>>=1){
      const t = idx+step;
      if(t<=this.n && this.f[t] < k){
        idx = t;
        k -= this.f[t];
      }
    }
    return idx+1;
  }
}
class KthMultiset {
  constructor(vals){
    this.values = Array.from(new Set(vals)).sort((a,b)=>a-b);
    this.index = new Map(this.values.map((v,i)=>[v,i+1]));
    this.ft = new Fenwick(this.values.length);
  }
  add(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, 1); }
  remove(x){ const i = this.index.get(x); if(i) this.ft.add(i, -1); }
  getKthSmallest(k){ const idx = this.ft.kth(k); return this.values[idx-1]; }
}
```

